        "Structure": {
          "Structure_Text": {
            "original": "The cracking is completely eliminated in the H230AM sample. The network segregation phases are completely dissolved in the H230AM after normal atmospheric heat treatment, and extensive precipitated phases with sizes of 166 ± 18 nm are uniformly distributed in the matrix. The precipitated phase in the H230AM is identified as ZrC carbide, which is distributed along cell and grain boundaries. The number densities of carbides in H230AM were counted to be 1.71 μm⁻². The average grain sizes of H230AM after heat treatment were 10.9 μm. The grain size of H230AM (10 μm) remains essentially unchanged after creep testing. After creep at 900 °C/65 MPa, three types of precipitation phases are present in H230AM: ZrC (retained from heat treatment), M₆C carbides (~380 nm) along grain boundaries, and plate-like Ni₇Zr₂ phase (~300 nm) within grain interior. The Ni₇Zr₂ phase has orientation relationship (001)_Ni₇Zr₂ // (111)_γ with the γ matrix. Unique matrix distortion stripes exist along (022) planes of γ matrix. The HAADF-STEM image shows a coherent interface between the Ni₇Zr₂ phase and γ matrix after thermal exposure. The Ni₇Zr₂ phase average size is 293 ± 58 nm and 289 ± 51 nm after thermal exposure at 900 °C for 50 h and 500 h, respectively.",
            "simplified": "As-printed: crack-free. After heat treatment (1200 °C/1 h/AC): ZrC carbides (166 ± 18 nm) uniformly distributed, number density 1.71 μm⁻². Grain size 10.9 μm. After creep at 900 °C/65 MPa: three precipitate phases — ZrC (retained), M₆C (~380 nm) at grain boundaries, Ni₇Zr₂ (~300 nm, plate-like) within grains. Matrix distortion stripes along (022) planes. Ni₇Zr₂ coherent with γ matrix, orientation (001)_Ni₇Zr₂ // (111)_γ."
          },
